# Testing with Vitest + React Testing Library + MSW v2

## Core Principle

Write the minimum tests that catch real bugs. Before each test, ask what user-facing behavior would break if untested — and whether another test already covers it. See `code-minimalism.mdc` for the broader ethos; this file owns test-specific application.

### Test File Size Guidelines

Past ~400 lines, review the file for redundant cases, irrelevant edge cases, and over-mocking before adding more tests.

## Goals & Philosophy
- Test **behavior over implementation details**; follow Arrange-Act-Assert
- Never change dependencies/lockfiles to "fix" tests
- Write tests for components with logic or user interactions
- Use descriptive test names; cover Happy, Invalid, and Boundary per the [H/I/B Pattern](#hib-pattern-quality-over-quantity) section
- Use `render`, `screen`, `waitFor` from `@/test/test-utils` (includes ReactQueryProvider)
- Interactive suites: `const user = userEvent.setup({ delay: null })` — avoids default typing delay in CI

## Test Pyramid Guidelines

Prefer integration tests for components with external dependencies (forms, API routes, auth flows); unit tests for pure functions, hooks, and isolated logic. No E2E infrastructure exists yet.

## Mocking Policy
**Only mock system boundaries** - not your own business logic:
- ✅ **Do mock**: External APIs, auth providers, time/randomness, network requests
- ❌ **Don't mock**: Your own functions, utilities, or business logic in integration tests
- Mock at the system boundary, not internal implementation

### Mock Service Worker (MSW v2)
- **No `src/mocks/` directory yet** — MSW is a dev dependency but global setup is deferred until a real HTTP boundary needs it (`vitest.setup.ts` comment). Supabase/auth boundaries use `vi.mock` at module level today.
- **When the first HTTP-boundary test ships:** recreate minimal global infra — `src/mocks/handlers.ts` (start with `export const handlers = []`), thin `src/mocks/server.ts`, wire `server.listen()` / `resetHandlers()` in `vitest.setup.ts`. Use per-test `server.use()` only for test-specific overrides on top of shared handlers.
- MSW is for testing only (not in production).

### Mocking Supabase
- Mock Supabase client only at the system boundary when necessary
- For integration tests, prefer using a test database over mocking
- Mock auth state for client components testing user-specific behavior

## Running Tests

| Context | Command |
|---------|---------|
| Default (agents, automation, one-shot) | `pnpm test` |
| Human local dev (watch) | `pnpm test:watch` |
| CI / pre-push (coverage) | `pnpm test:ci` |
| Single file (no coverage) | `pnpm test:file -- <path>` |

```bash
# Full suite, non-watch (default)
pnpm test

# Watch mode — human dev only
pnpm test:watch

# CI / pre-push with coverage thresholds
pnpm test:ci

# Targeted run without coverage
pnpm test:file -- src/utils/foo.unit.test.ts
```

## H/I/B Pattern: Quality Over Quantity

Test these three scenarios with **1-3 representative cases each**:

- **Happy Path**: The main success flow (1-2 tests)
  - Example: User submits valid form → sees success message
  - Complex flows may need more than one success case

- **Invalid Input**: Common failure cases (1-3 tests)
  - Example: User submits empty required field → sees validation error
  - Test distinct error messages users will see, but don't test every validation rule individually

- **Boundary Case**: Edge cases that matter to users (1-2 tests)
  - Example: Empty list displays "no items" message
  - Don't test: empty string, whitespace, unicode, emoji, special chars unless specifically relevant

If a function or component needs more than ~15 tests, investigate for redundancy.

**Exception**: Complex, security-critical, or high-risk code may justify more comprehensive testing. The goal is to catch real bugs efficiently, not to hit arbitrary limits.

### What NOT to Test

#### Skip These:
- **Framework behavior**: Don't test that React renders, Next.js routes work, or React Query caches
- **Third-party libraries**: Don't test that Supabase queries work or Radix/shadcn primitives render correctly
- **Trivial code**: Don't test simple getters/setters, pass-through functions, or type definitions
- **Every validation rule**: If you have 10 form validations, test 1-2 representative cases, not all 10
- **Every edge case**: Don't test empty string AND whitespace AND unicode AND emoji AND special chars
- **Combinations of edge cases**: Don't test "empty string with whitespace" or "unicode emoji"
- **Implementation details**: Don't test internal state, private methods, or CSS classes — ESLint bans `toHaveClass` and class-string `querySelector` probes in `*.test.*` (allowlisted `sr-only` a11y pin on admin users-table loading label)
- **Multiple ways to trigger the same behavior**: Don't test keyboard AND mouse if they do the same thing
- **Render smoke tests for components visible in normal dev flow**: See the render-only rule under UI Components & Pages

#### Test These Instead:
- **User-facing behavior**: What the user sees and experiences
- **Business logic**: Calculations, validations, transformations unique to your app
- **Error states**: What happens when critical operations fail
- **Integration points**: How your components/functions work together
- **Accessibility**: Screen reader support, keyboard navigation for interactive elements

### Over-Testing

Avoid many near-duplicate edge-case or validation micro-tests, or asserting implementation details (internal state, private methods, CSS classes) instead of user-visible behavior. Prefer 1-2 representative cases per category over exhaustive permutations. Shipped examples: [`src/utils/extract-auth-form-error.unit.test.ts`](../../src/utils/extract-auth-form-error.unit.test.ts) (focused unit coverage) and [`src/components/login-form.integration.test.tsx`](../../src/components/login-form.integration.test.tsx) (behavior-level integration).

## Test Authoring by Type

### UI Components & Pages
- If a component needs more than ~10 tests, investigate for redundancy. There is no minimum — some components need zero tests.
- Use Vitest + React Testing Library
- Use role/name/text queries (e.g., `getByRole`, `getByText`)
- Include at least one user interaction (e.g., `userEvent.click`)
- Assert accessibility attributes for interactive elements only
- No snapshot tests — lint-enforced; write explicit assertions instead
- Test loading and error states only if they're critical to UX
- **Render-only tests** (assert only that expected content appears, no interaction or branch): allowed *only* for surfaces unreachable during normal development — error boundaries and fallback states. Static content components (marketing sections, config-driven copy) get no render tests; TypeScript and visual review cover them. Everything else must assert behavior.
- **Don't test**: Every possible prop combination, internal state changes, CSS classes

### API Routes & Handlers
- If a route needs more than ~6 tests, investigate for redundancy. There is no minimum.
- Test success path with valid input
- Test authentication/authorization failures
- Validate request/response shapes for key scenarios
- Assert proper HTTP status codes
- Mock external HTTP with MSW only after global infra ships (see MSW section above); until then mock at the module boundary (`vi.mock`) as the shipped client-logs integration test does
- **Don't test**: Every validation error scenario, database errors (unless route-specific behavior)

### Hooks & Utilities
- If a hook or utility needs more than ~8 tests, investigate for redundancy. There is no minimum.
- Test happy path with typical inputs
- Test common failure cases
- Test critical edge cases (null, empty, boundary values)
- Mock external boundaries only (network requests, browser APIs)
- Ensure type safety is maintained
- **Don't test**: Every possible input combination, TypeScript type checking
- **Exception**: Security-critical utilities (like sanitization, validation) may need exhaustive edge case coverage

## Coverage Requirements

**Enforced:** 80% minimum thresholds (lines, functions, branches, statements) via [`vitest.config.ts`](../../vitest.config.ts). `pnpm test:ci` runs `vitest run --coverage` — failures block CI and pre-push.

**In scope:** app logic under `src/`, check scripts under `scripts/checks/`, build guards under `scripts/build/`, custom lint rules under `eslint-rules/`, and admin CLI lib modules under `scripts/admin/lib/`. **Excluded from denominator:** see the authoritative exclude list in [`vitest.config.ts`](../../vitest.config.ts) — thin page/layout shells, vendored UI primitives, admin CLI entry shims, untested check scripts (F186), and other items called out there with comments.

Before merge: run `pnpm test:ci`. If thresholds fail, add targeted high-value tests — not per-file 80% crusades. If useful tests cannot reach 80%, revisit threshold or exclude set (PM decision)—do not pad with filler.

## Performance & Reliability
- Keep tests fast — Vitest flags tests slower than its `slowTestThreshold` (default 300ms) in run output
- Avoid flaky tests - if a test is unreliable, fix or quarantine it
- Use `describe.skip()` with "QUARANTINE: reason" for temporarily disabled tests
- Always link to a GitHub issue when quarantining tests
- The `seminova-test/no-unquarantined-skips` ESLint rule enforces the quarantine convention

